<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">French conquest of Algeria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> began with the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> from its Ottoman-era regency in 1830 and continued for decades. Constantine fell in 1837, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir-abdelkader">Emir Abdelkader</a> led resistance in the west until his surrender in 1847, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabylia">Kabylia</a> was subdued in 1857, with later revolts such as that of 1871 put down. Algeria was then ruled as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and settled by Europeans until its independence in 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-conquest-of-algeria.jpg" alt="French conquest of Algeria" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horace Vernet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_prise_de_Constantine_1837_par_Horace_Vernet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The coastal and mountainous parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> were controlled by the Regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, which was nominally part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> but acted independently of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>. The dey exercised direct control only in and around Algiers, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a> called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">Beyliks</a> in the west, centre and east. Much of the interior was effectively under local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and Arab leaders, and the inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> was only claimed by the dey. In 1827 an argument between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-dey">Hussein Dey</a> and the French consul over debts, and over French attempts to fortify storehouses at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaba">Bône</a> and La Calle, escalated into a naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Admiral Duperré took an armada of 635 ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a>, and General de Bourmont landed 34,000 soldiers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidi-fredj">Sidi Ferruch</a> on 14 June 1830. The French defeated the dey's army at Staouéli on 19 June and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> on 5 July. The dey accepted capitulation and went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, ending 313 years of Ottoman rule there. Governor Clauzel then introduced a civil administration, recruited zouaves and helped form a company to settle European farmers on the land, which set off a land rush.</p><p>Resistance centred on Ahmed Bey at Constantine and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir-abdelkader">Emir Abdelkader</a> in the west. In May 1837 General Bugeaud signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tafna">Treaty of Tafna</a> with Abdelkader, which effectively recognised his control of much of the interior. French forces took Constantine later in 1837, after Clauzel's attack had failed in November 1836; the commander Damrémont was killed in the siege. Abdelkader renewed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> in 1839 and waged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war through 1840. Driven into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in 1842, he fought on until the Moroccan government, pressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after the Franco-Moroccan War, attacked him, and he surrendered in 1847.</p><p>The conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> began in March 1844 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biskra">Biskra</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis-Philippe</a>, duc d'Aumale, a twenty-two-year-old general, and proceeded partly through agreements with local tribes. The Mokrani Revolt of 1871 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabylia">Kabylia</a> spread widely, and by April 250 tribes, nearly a third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>'s population, had risen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> secured its Saharan borders only in 1903.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>According to several historians, the French methods reached genocidal proportions, and war, famine and disease killed between 500,000 and 1 million Algerians in the first three decades, out of an estimated 3 million. French losses from 1831 to 1851 were 3,336 killed in battle and 92,329 dead of disease in hospital. Various governments and scholars have considered the conquest a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. The colonists numbered roughly 130,000 in 1871 and controlled most of the arable land; the number grew to over a million before the century ended.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French conquest of Algeria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
